# UNVERIFIED — pending debrief
# Adversarial case, M-package-layout (panel 099; was M8a step 1, panel 031).
#
# **THIS CASE CHANGED SIDES.** It used to pin the refusal of `use shapes/geom`
# itself: a path was the design space a bare identifier forecloses, and both a
# dotted and a slashed name got one message. Panel 099 landed the path, so what
# is left to pin is what a path may NOT be — and each of these is refused for a
# reason that would otherwise be a silence:
#
# - a DOT is not the separator. The message names the spelling that works,
#   because the author who writes `geom.shapes` knows exactly what they meant.
# - a CLIMB has no spelling at all, and that is a design decision rather than a
#   slip (R4): every `use` starts at the directory of the file you compile, so a
#   module above it belongs to a different program. One message answers `.`,
#   `..`, `./x` and `../x` alike — four spellings of one intent.
# - a SEPARATOR WITH NOTHING AFTER IT names the two modules it sits between.
#
# Not here, and deliberately: `use a / b`, which lexes as the same three tokens
# as `use a/b` and is legal — it is one `use` of `a`, and what refuses it is the
# unused-binding rule rather than the grammar. Adjacency is the rule, and
# `selfhost/module_paths.hero` carries the test that keeps the compiler's two
# readers of it agreeing.
use geom.shapes  #~ module_path_has_no_parts

use ../token  #~ module_path_climbs

use lex/..  #~ module_path_climbs

use shapes/  #~ module_path_wants_a_part

function main()
    print(0)
